From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Date: Thu, 26 May 94 13:16:48 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-279] AI: NeuronicComputer/Microtubules/Hameroff

As far as I know, microtubules are essential in transportation of various
molecules throughout neurons.  They function much like a conveyor belt, with
small microtubule-associated-proteins (MAPs) riding along them while carrying
various large proteins.  One interesting thing is that the microtubules have
poles, or specificity with respect to the direction in which they carry
specific cargo (some stuff goes toward the positive, some toward the negative).
In axons the tubules are all oriented in the same direction, whereas in
dendrites their directions seem to be fairly scattered.  Such self-organization
is simple to construct, and may be induced by the action of an enzyme
called Tau, which has been shown to be involved in axonal growth.  Anyway, this
arrangement may have a simple function.  In an axon, where the microtubules are
oriented, specific molecules will tend to move in only one direction, allowing
enzymes constructed at the cell body to be transported downstream and
intracellular retrograde messengers upstream.  There's plenty of biochemical
motivation for such transport.  In dendrites however, where microtubules are
not oriented, the tendency will be to equalize concentrations of molecules by a
process not unlike entropy.  Thus, the distribution of microtubule orientation
reflects the functionality of cell structures - the axon is a directional
device, the dendrites are an information gathering area.  I would guess that at
the end of an axon, where it splits into collaterals, microtubule orientations
become a little more scattered as in dendrites.

Anyway, as far as computational function, anything that has an effect on
synaptic transmission is a candidate.  Of course, that includes just about
everything in the cell.  But microtubules are especially important in
delivering enzymes to synapses, and thus can control the rate of reactions
there.  Varying microtubule densities to different parts of a dendritic tree is
like varying a gain on the "weights" of the affected synapses.  So you have
another structural variable to consider in addition to the dozens already
potentially involved in long term memory...

Paul


